WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.910
This is an oversimplification, but here's
how a movie gets made: actors film scenes

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.269
by saying their lines, and then the director
and editors assemble a cut using the best

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:10.540
takes of each scene.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.450
Sometimes a messed up take ends up in the
final film because it still works with the

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.420
story, but other times, bloopers just have
no business being anywhere near the movie.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.189
Here are some bloopers and outtakes from movies
that never made it into the final cut for

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.400
some very easily understandable reasons.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.400
Suicide Squad

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.750
In director David Ayer's manic, cartoonish
villain-o-rama Suicide Squad, the usually

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.570
heroic Will Smith plays very much against
type as Deadshot, a character who's both a

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.520
hitman and an expert sharpshooter.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.879
In other words, he's someone who knows his
guns.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.760
So he would never do this:

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.400
"Ahhh!"

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:48.680
"You guys are safe."

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.020
A shot of Will Smith bungling the process
of loading a gun might make a funny moment

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.940
in a different film, but it never would have
worked for the character named after his ability

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.949
to shoot things.

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:01.309
Bad Moms

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:02.949
NFL defenseman J.J.

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:07.460
Watt has started making the move into showbiz,
and he's a pretty funny guy who usually seems

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.900
as comfortable onscreen as he does smashing
into other behemoths on the gridiron.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.620
In Bad Moms, he plays a soccer coach who bears
some of the brunt of Mila Kunis' character's

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.770
rage—and in one take, she so convincingly
bursts into Watt's office that the otherwise

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.170
macho dude seems genuinely scared.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.880
"Gwendolyn made me do it!"

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:26.900
"What the f--- did you say?!"

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.430
"You kicked the s--- out of that door!"

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.170
Unfortunately, the blooper had to be cut.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.830
You can't have your movie's tough guy screaming
like one of the kids he coaches.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.810
That Awkward Moment

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.770
This millennial comedy is all about the cringe-inducing
moments we all seem to put ourselves through.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:45.990
But the most awkward moment in the movie occurs
in a blooper when just about every framed

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.080
poster in a conference room scene comes loose
and falls down.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.780
"Hah!"

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.220
It wasn't planned, and as fittingly awkward
as it may have been, it kind of ruins a movie's

00:01:58.221 --> 00:02:02.770
credibility if the entire set starts falling
apart onscreen.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.020
Scandal

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.520
Most modern Americans carry their smartphone
with them wherever they go.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.590
Even actors actively working on a set carry
their phones around with them—out of view

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.140
of the camera and with the ringer turned off,
of course.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.050
Well, they're supposed to turn the ringer
off.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.750
While shooting a scene for Scandal, Guillermo
Diaz gets a bit embarrassed when he's interrupted

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.900
by the sound of his own phone going off.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.630
"We knew this was coming, Liv."

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:28.820
"Sorry, lemme check my text really quick!"

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.050
Red Eye

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:34.020
In Red Eye, Cillian Murphy plays a psycho
terrorizing a woman on a plane.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.790
It's a tense, pulse-pounding, exhausting thriller
with few light-hearted moments, and you get

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.650
the real sense that Cillian's character is
so crazy he can't be stopped.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.650
You tell the flight attendant and your dad
dies."

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.120
That means that this charming bit with a little
girl threatening to beat up Murphy just didn't

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.120
fit in.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.320
"Is that guy bugging you?

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.260
Because I can take him out in a New York Minute."

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.430
The girl is still in the final film, but her
apparently improvised line is nowhere to be

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.430
seen.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:03.430
Thanks for watching!

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.510
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.660
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

